// A pair VALUE piped into a two-parameter function REFUSES (E_TypeMismatch —
// the function-arity face): a pair is one argument, and the parameter lists
// pair arity for arity. Until 2026-09-27 a unification rule decomposed any
// tuple against the parameters ("parameters ARE tuples") and the program
// checked clean, then trapped at the indirect call, because the emit never
// carried that rule into the calling convention (RED-first on boot b145b836:
// exit 0, `indirect call type mismatch` at run). A product BUILT AT THE SITE
// is the other shape and it runs: `(1, 2) |> add` is the merge, `add(1, 2)`,
// one rule the judgment and the lowering both read (V2, 2026-10-06 —
// mn-product-merges-into-stage). What crosses a binding is a value, and a
// value fills one slot until `Hβ.lower.parameter-product-calling-convention`
// lands; this file turns green only by that landing, never by re-admitting
// the decomposition at the type layer.
fn add(a, b) = a + b

fn main() = {
  let p = (1, 2)
  p |> add
}
